From ea4e568fd3bcb360fef11d219560479537ef7e28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wray-lee <wray-lee@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:16:04 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] feat(security): per-agent autonomy for named agents
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Named agents all ran with the global autonomy policy: SessionManager built
every agent runtime's tools from the global config.autonomy and shared the
single global SecurityPolicy, so a guest agent bound to one chat peer had the
same shell and path permissions as the main agent.

Give agents.list[] entries an optional "autonomy" object with the same keys as
the top-level block. When present it replaces the global autonomy for that
agent; omitted keys fall back to the secure AutonomyConfig defaults instead of
inheriting the (possibly yolo) global values, and an unknown level is rejected
at config load. Such an agent always gets a dedicated runtime that owns its own
SecurityPolicy and RateTracker, built from its autonomy and scoped to its own
workspace, and its sessions use that policy.

Tools that would run work under the global policy instead of the agent's, or
that reach outside its workspace, are withheld from an agent with its own
autonomy:
  - spawn: the SubagentManager is bound to the global config, so subagents
    execute under the global autonomy.
  - schedule: creates cron shell jobs that run under the scheduler's global
    shell policy.
  - image_info: opens any absolute path with no workspace/policy check, so it
    is a host-path metadata oracle.
  - git: writes to the repository (commit, checkout, reset, ...) regardless of
    read_only intent.
  - screenshot: captures the host screen, leaking information outside the
    workspace.
  - MCP tools: initMcpTools spawns each configured server as a child process
    and proxies its tools without consulting the agent's policy.
The cron_* tools are not registered in the agent tool set at all, and delegate
is kept because it performs a single tool-less completion (no shell, no file
access).

The global configuration surface is closed too, because it is reachable from a
bound chat peer's message:
  - /config is refused: get/set/apply/reload read or rewrite the shared
    config.json (provider keys, channel tokens, the autonomy/security blocks)
    under the global policy.
  - /capabilities no longer reads the shared config.json; it reports only the
    agent's own runtime tools.
  - /model no longer persists agents.defaults.model.primary for such an agent,
    and its interactive provider/model menu no longer reads the shared
    config.json (it falls back to the public model list instead of the global
    provider keys).
  - /export is refused: it takes an arbitrary absolute path and
    truncates/creates it, a whole-host file-write primitive reachable from a
    bound chat peer. The remaining slash commands are session-local (model,
    think, verbose, reasoning, usage, cost, queue, tts, focus/dock/activation/
    send, session ttl, context, memory, skills, debug, stop, compact, doctor),
    or operate on subagents that are unavailable to the agent because spawn is
    withheld (/subagents spawn, /steer, /tell, /agents, /kill, /poll report the
    manager is disabled). /bash, /approve, /exec and /elevated run through the
    agent's own shell tool and policy, so the confinement below applies to them.
  - Telegram /bind is refused when the peer is routed to an agent with its own
    autonomy: otherwise /bind clear or /bind <other> would move the peer to the
    default or another (unrestricted) agent.
  - The runtime config used to build the agent carries the agent's own autonomy
    (makeAgentConfig), so Agent.fromConfigWithProfile derives exec_security/
    exec_ask, allowed_paths and multimodal_unrestricted from it instead of the
    global yolo block.
  - The per-agent shell policy is confined to the agent's workspace. The command
    is word-split on every byte the shell treats as IFS whitespace (space, tab,
    newline, vertical tab, form feed) and every resulting argument that names a
    path (absolute, `..` traversal or `~`) is rejected; a control byte anywhere
    in the command is rejected outright, because /bin/sh splits on it into
    arguments the check would otherwise not see (`cat x\t/etc/passwd` runs
    `cat x /etc/passwd`). `$` parameter expansion (`$IFS`, `$HOME`, `$1`, `$@`,
    `${...}`, `$'...'`, ...) and unquoted glob/brace metacharacters are
    rejected, because the shell expands them at execution time into text the
    argument check never sees (`cat $IFS/etc/passwd` becomes `cat /etc/passwd`,
    `cat {.,..}/etc/passwd` becomes `cat ../etc/passwd`). Commands that execute
    code taken from the workspace are refused outright: the package managers
    and their run/build verbs (`npm`, `pnpm`, `yarn`, `npx`, `bun`, `bunx`,
    `deno`, `cargo`), and the git subcommands that run a script (`git bisect`,
    `git submodule`, `git filter-branch`, `git filter-repo`, plus git `-c`
    config injection and `!`-aliases, which the git argument check already
    rejects). This is a best-effort argument check applied to every command, not
    a filesystem jail, and it is defence-in-depth for the common no-sandbox case
    rather than a substitute for an external sandbox. The global policy keeps
    its current behaviour.

Outbound attachments are confined too. Channels parse the agent's reply text
for legacy [DOCUMENT:/abs/path] / [FILE:..] / [IMAGE:..] markers and upload any
absolute path (curl --form file=@path) with no workspace or policy check; this
does not go through a tool call, so it also affected read_only. When the agent
has its own autonomy the reply is now filtered at the session boundary — in
both processMessageStreaming and handleLocalSlashCommand (the Telegram
callback-menu path), through one helper — so a local attachment target must be
an absolute path that resolves (realpath, symlinks followed) inside the agent's
workspace or one of its own allowed_paths (isResolvedPathAllowed). Relative
targets are refused as well, because the channel uploader resolves them against
its own process cwd rather than the workspace, so a relative marker could pass
this check while the channel uploads a different file (e.g. the real
config.json). Escaping or relative markers are dropped and logged, and remote
http(s) URLs are unaffected. Filtering at the session boundary covers every
channel that uploads markers (Telegram, Max, Signal, ...) with one check. Slack
never uploads attachment targets (it returns NotSupported for payloads that
carry attachments), so it needed no change.

The inbound multimodal path is closed as well: an agent with its own autonomy
no longer gets the platform temp dir in the multimodal allowed_dirs, so a bound
peer cannot make it read a host file under the temp dir by sending
[IMAGE:/tmp/<file>] — a tool-less read outside the workspace that also applied
to read_only. Agents without their own autonomy keep the temp dir.

Docs (en + zh) now state the guarantees exactly: the supervised shell
confinement is a best-effort argument check, not a jail; read_only is the
recommended level for untrusted guests (it disables all tool calls); and the
attachment and slash-command restrictions are listed.

Two latent problems found while reviewing this change are fixed as well: an
unknown autonomy level is now rejected before parseJson writes any field, so
Config.load no longer keeps a half-parsed config that silently drops the rest;
and Config.save no longer double-frees the serialized agent api_key strings when
the loop errors part-way.

Backslash escapes outside single quotes are refused in the confined shell
check: the shell unescapes them after the check runs on the raw text, so
`cat \/etc/passwd` reaches /etc/passwd. The delegate tool is also withheld,
since it runs any configured agent's provider with the operator's key.

The confined shell check also refuses every byte the shell would reinterpret
after the check: a confined command may contain only letters, digits, plain
spaces, the separators `;`, `|`, `&&`, `||` and the punctuation `-_./,:=+@%^`.
Quotes, redirections, escapes, expansions and globs are rejected, so
`cat ''/etc/passwd`, `cat '..'/x` and `cat </etc/passwd` no longer pass, and
the find write predicates (-execdir, -okdir, -delete, -fprint*, -fls) are
refused. `nullclaw agent --agent <name>` now applies the profile's autonomy
(policy, confinement and withheld tools) instead of the global block.
